# Line endings are LF in the repo AND in the working tree, everywhere.
#
# This file exists because not having it broke the site. core.autocrlf=true is
# set globally on the dev machine, so with no .gitattributes git converted the
# whole working tree to CRLF on checkout. Every generator in scripts/ writes
# with newline:"\n" and matches on "\n" — so sync-example-links stopped
# recognising its own output, appended a second copy of the "see a finished one"
# block to twenty of twenty-one tool pages, and then reported "0 written, 21
# already current" on every subsequent run. It shipped to production that way.
#
# The near miss was worse than the duplication: with <\/p>\n unable to match
# </p>\r\n, the lazy [\s\S]*? in that regex runs past the intended block until
# it finds a bare \n somewhere further down the file, so a strip could have
# taken real page content with it.
#
# eol=lf rather than text=auto alone: auto normalises what is COMMITTED but
# still hands Windows a CRLF working tree, which is exactly the state that
# caused this. The generators are the primary consumer of these files, not an
# editor, and they want LF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary — never touch these.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.xlsx binary

# CSV is CRLF by specification (RFC 4180) and these open in Excel.
*.csv text eol=crlf
